    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Education-Support-Lead_0157977-2": {
      "title": "Education Support Lead",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-15\nPosted: Posted Yesterday\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term until October 2027    Exciting opportunity to be an Education Support Lead within the Faculty of Medicine and Health      Base Salary starting at $117,133 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Faculty of Medicine and Health is seeking an experienced and collaborative Education Support Lead to oversee the delivery of education support services and ensure the effective administration of learning and teaching activities.  Supporting a portfolio of academic programs, the role provides leadership to education support staff while maintaining a strong operational focus across curriculum administration, assessment, compliance, reporting and student support activities. Working closely with academic and professional staff, you will coordinate education processes, support continuous improvement initiatives and contribute to the delivery of a high-quality student experience.  This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced education professional who enjoys leading people, improving processes and building strong stakeholder relationships in a dynamic and complex environment.  Your key responsibilities will be to:   lead and coordinate the delivery of education support services across the work area, ensuring effective and consistent administrative support for learning and teaching activities  supervise and support education support staff, providing coaching, guidance and performance development  build and maintain productive relationships with academic, teaching and professional staff to support education priorities and operational requirements  provide advice and guidance on University policies, procedures and education administration processes  oversee teaching administration activities, including curriculum support, assessments, examinations, results processing, student evaluations, scholarships and student case management processes  coordinate program evaluation activities and support continuous improvement initiatives to enhance education delivery and student outcomes  manage education systems, records and reporting requirements, ensuring data accuracy, compliance and effective use of University platforms  oversee the development and maintenance of learning and teaching resources, including online learning environments such as Canvas  support the onboarding and training of academic and sessional teaching staff in relevant systems and education processes  coordinate the implementation of Faculty and University education projects and initiatives  review and analyse data to support operational planning, reporting and decision making  contribute to communities of practice and collaborative initiatives across Education Support teams  identify opportunities to improve processes, streamline service delivery and enhance the staff and student experience  undertake other duties and project work appropriate to the classification level.    About you   You are an experienced education professional with strong organisational and leadership skills, a commitment to service excellence and the ability to build effective working relationships across a complex environment. You thrive in roles that combine people leadership with hands on operational delivery and enjoy working collaboratively to achieve shared outcomes.  To be successful, you will have:  tertiary qualifications in a relevant discipline or an equivalent combination of training and experience  demonstrated experience in education administration, preferably within a higher education environment  experience leading or supervising staff and contributing to a positive, high performing team culture  experience supporting key education administration processes, including curriculum, assessment and student related activities  knowledge of university policies, procedures and administrative frameworks relevant to learning and teaching  experience using learning management systems and student administration systems, including platforms such as Canvas  highly developed communication and stakeholder engagement skills, with the ability to work effectively with a diverse range of staff  strong analytical, problem solving and decision making skills  excellent organisational skills, with the ability to manage competing priorities and meet deadlines  a commitment to continuous improvement and delivering high quality services.    Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   Work Rights: you must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    Pre-employment checks   Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University. Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.  EEO statement    At the University of Sydney, our shared values include diversity and inclusion and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community which reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this commitment through our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.   How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.  For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Ajaypal Singh, Recruitment Operations by email to ajaypal.singh@sydney.edu.au.  © The University of Sydney  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment.    Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 21 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Forest-Lodge/Senior-Business-Analyst_0154395-1": {
      "title": "Senior Business Analyst",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time, fixed term maternity leave cover until 7th April 2028 Improve and optimize finance reporting and planning processes by capturing requirements Base Salary HEO8, $131,108 p.a.+ 17% superannuation       About the opportunity    The Senior Business Analyst will utilize technical and interpersonal skills to effectively engage with stakeholders to capture requirements and document existing and new processes. This position will support the lifecycle of the Financial Planning, Analytics & Commercial team’s reporting and planning projects by leading the tasks of requirements gathering, report wireframes, the writing and execution of testing scripts, rollout, and support.  The position will also be involved in AI projects around reporting key financial figures by writing User Stories, defining Personas and Journey paths. The position requires both an enthusiasm for change, an interest in Business Intelligence systems and experience in reporting and planning projects. Furthermore, this position is expected to assist in troubleshooting issues during the go live “hyper care” phase. As well as transitioning the support from project hyper care to standard BAU support.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   build effective relationships and work collaboratively with all staff in the Finance PSU and staff in other areas to identify and develop initiatives that optimise processes manage the development and implementation of test plans (System and UAT testing) to ensure dashboard/reporting or planning requirements have been delivered in line with the Business Intelligence strategy and timeframes lead workshops and meetings with project and stakeholder teams to identify, document and implement business improvements relating to Finance Business Intelligence and Planning and Budgeting processes collaborate with project managers to support the effective delivery of large Financial Services projects. For smaller internal team projects, develop project plans and track progress through to implementation drive the rollout and adoption of projects by developing and providing knowledge base content, user training and ongoing system education relating to BI or Planning modules.     About you   bachelor’s degree in a relevant field (Accounting degree preferred, or strong experience in Finance/Planning Reporting projects) membership of Institute of Chartered Accountants or Certified Practicing Accountants Australia preferred previous experience in financial planning and budgeting systems experience in Financial Reporting projects and systems ability to communicate effectively with analytical and problem-solving skills.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.    Pre-employment checks and declarations      Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.     EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Kevin Lim, Recruitment Operations by email to Victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au .   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 25 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Health--Safety-and-Wellbeing-Director-Systems-and-Enablement_0158068-2": {
      "title": "Health, Safety and Wellbeing Director Systems and Enablement",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Competitive remuneration package including 17% superannuation   About the team The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity The HSW Director Systems and Enablement is a senior University leadership role. In this position, you will be accountable for setting and driving the strategic direction of the University’s HSW systems, governance, assurance, reporting, and digital enablement functions. Operating at a senior strategic level with influence extending to University Executive and Senate, the Director shapes a proactive agenda, provides trusted advice to senior leaders, and ensures sustained organisational focus on strategic priorities, risk management, and performance. The role plays a critical leadership function in embedding a cohesive and forward-looking approach to health, safety and wellbeing risks across the institution. Your key responsibilities will be to: lead the design and development of HSW Systems and Enablement function strategic objectives and goals for the University team leadership and budget management set and evolve the HSW Systems and Enablement operating model, ensuring clarity of roles, service offerings, and integration across systems, governance, assurance, and reporting functions set and evolve the governance and reporting framework for the HSW function lead the University wide assurance/audit program provide strategic oversight and direction for HSW systems and digital enablement and continuous improvement lead the University’s HSW Program Management activities. This role will suit a contemporary HSW leader who combines strong systems and governance capability with the ability to set direction and lead digital and technology enabled change, while bringing people with them through complex organisational change.   About you graduate degree/ professional qualifications or equivalent experience in a WHS or related discipline extensive experience within Work Health and Safety field with a demonstrated experience of relevant legislation and policies demonstrated leadership in developing an effective and sustainable safety culture and conceptualising, developing and implementing practical compliance and safety programs extensive leadership experience leading multi-disciplinary Work Health and Safety specialist professionals within large, complex and highly regulated organisations demonstrated experience in applying H&S Safety Management strategies in complex environments advanced knowledge of Risk Management practices and the application of government legislation, codes of practice and Australian standards advanced knowledge of relevant Australian Safety Standards as well as relevant Safe Work Australia, SafeWork NSW and Workcover publications extensive experience with the development of HSMS systems and provision of technical supporting tools. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. How to apply The Safe Step is assisting the University of Sydney with this appointment. Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. For further information and a confidential conversation about the role please contact Stephen Coldicutt on scoldicutt@thesafestep.com.au or 02 8256 2529. Pre-employment checks and declarations Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion . © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Solution-Designer_0146190-2": {
      "title": "Solution Designer",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nWork across applications, integrations, cloud,   infrastructure   and security in a varied solution design role   Help shape technology solutions that support the University’s teaching,   research   and strategic priorities   Salary   $131,108   to   $147,858   + 17% Superannuation   Are you an experienced Solution Designer who enjoys turning complex business needs into practical technology solutions? Do you thrive at the intersection of business and ICT, working with stakeholders and technical teams to design solutions that can be successfully delivered?   Solve by Talent is partnering with the University of Sydney to recruit a Solution Designer to develop robust and practical technology solutions across key ICT and   university   initiatives. Working within the ICT Solution Architecture team, you will help shape solutions that support the University’s strategic   and operational   priorities   to suppor t its core   education   and research   goals   This is an exciting opportunity to contribute to a broad range of strategic   and operational   projects while working across a large and complex technology environment within one of the world’s leading universities.   Why join the University of Sydney?   Join one of Australia’s leading universities at a pivotal point of change, where technology is evolving to better support student learning, empower teaching excellence, and enable research delivery at global scale.       ICT is central to delivering the University’s academic mission. Working across faculties, disciplines, and research communities   and operational teams , you will   contribute to   the platforms, services, and partnerships that enable student success, strengthen teaching, and accelerate research impact.       This work spans both the critical services that keep the University running every day and the ongoing improvement of systems and experiences.       With strong investment and clear direction   in a digital   stratgey ,   you’ll   operate   in a large and complex environment where your contribution   is part of a   visible, meaningful impact across the University.       You’ll   collaborate across academic and professional communities to help shape a more connected and capable University, supporting teaching excellence, research impact, and student success.     The Opportunity   As a Solution Designer, you will work from business and project requirements to   determine   practical technology solutions within the University’s enterprise architecture standards and guardrails. You will develop and document high-level and detailed solution designs, work closely with business stakeholders and technical specialists, and provide technical leadership throughout solution delivery.   You will collaborate across application, infrastructure, cloud,   security   and other   technology   teams, preparing designs for stakeholder and governance review and helping delivery teams implement approved solutions. You will also contribute to solution design standards,   templates   and strategies, and share knowledge across the team.   You’ll   join a collaborative and supportive environment where diverse perspectives are valued, with flexible working arrangements and opportunities to contribute to   impactful   projects .   This role is ideal for a technically strong and collaborative professional who can translate business needs into practical solutions, communicate confidently with a wide range of stakeholders, and manage competing priorities in a complex project environment.   What   you’ll   do   Develop high-level and detailed solution designs for ICT and business initiatives   Work with business stakeholders and project teams to determine   appropriate solution   options from agreed requirements   Design and document how applications, integrations and technology components work together   Collaborate with application specialists, infrastructure, cloud   operations   and security teams throughout solution delivery   Prepare solution designs for stakeholder,   governance   and peer review, and communicate risks,   issues   and decisions   Provide technical leadership and guidance to delivery teams throughout projects   Contribute to solution design standards,   templates   and strategies, while mentoring and sharing knowledge with other team members   What   we’re   looking for   Proven experience developing and documenting high-level and detailed solution designs   Strong understanding of applications, application integration, technology   stacks   and cloud environments   Understanding of non-functional requirements and how they influence solution design, including security considerations   Good   communication   skills with   the ability to work effectively across business and technical teams   Experience working across complex ICT projects and managing competing priorities within agreed   timeframes   Experience working in both agile and waterfall delivery environments, with a relevant postgraduate qualification and/or equivalent combination of training and experience   W illingness to   adapt   to , and   apply   new   and emerging   techniques   and skills   in your   work   practices .     What’s   in it for you?   At the University of Sydney, we support our staff with a range of benefits designed to enhance your career,   wellbeing   and work–life balance, including:   17% superannuation   to support your financial future   G enerous parental leave entitlements.   Competitive salary with salary packaging options   Flexible and hybrid working   arrangements   Career development and learning opportunities   Health and wellbeing programs including   Fitness Passport   Access to a vibrant, globally recognised university community   How to apply   If you're looking for an opportunity to contribute to meaningful work while building your career in a world-class university environment, we would love to hear from you.  Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the  Apply  button at the top of the page.    For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your  Workday  account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. Accessibility and inclusion  The University of Sydney is committed to creating an inclusive recruitment experience for all candidates. If you require adjustments or additional support at any stage of the recruitment process, we encourage you to let us know.  This may include adjustments to the interview format, additional time, breaks, accessible interview locations, receiving materials in advance, or any other practical support that will help you participate comfortably.  For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact  camila.garcia@sydney.edu.au  to learn more about this role.  ​   EEO statement       At the University of Sydney, our shared values are  trust, accountability and excellence  and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to  diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of  Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People ,  women ,  people living with a disability ,  people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as  LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.       We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer.  Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .     Pre-employment checks  Your employment is conditional upon the completion of all role required pre-employment or background checks in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process.  Sponsorship / work rights for Australia  You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Wednesday 28 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Specialist-Health-and-Wellbeing_0158080-1": {
      "title": "Specialist Health and Wellbeing",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base Salary from $131,108 plus 17% superannuation About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity As Specialist Health and Wellbeing , you will lead the end-to-end management of health and wellbeing activities and program initiatives. Working in close partnership with the Psychosocial Safety, Health and Wellbeing, HSW Systems and Enablement and HSW Partnering teams, this role will ensure health and wellbeing programs and initiatives are aligned with enterprise HSMS frameworks, effectively translated into communication, systems and tools, and consistently embedded across operational areas. In this role you will support the development and delivery of preventative based tools, resources and initiatives that form part of the Health and Wellbeing Program, and support local area implementation and embedding activities, ensuring robust awareness and engagement across Faculties, Schools, Portfolios and Business Units. Your key responsibilities will be to: design, develop and coordinate health and wellbeing education programs and initiatives manage the University Wellbeing Champion Network manage the coordination, communication and implementation of all learning and development offerings that form part of the Health and Wellbeing Program coordinate and promote health promotion initiatives, managing associated partnerships and contracts with external providers provide subject matter expertise into psychosocial, health and wellbeing communications, building awareness of offerings across the University community partner with HSW teams to embed psychosocial, health and wellbeing offerings across diverse University portfolios. About you Graduate qualifications in psychology, organisational development, learning and development, or occupational health and safety oy or equivalent combination of training and experience  experience managing multiple projects and activities according to agreed deliverables experience designing end-end initiatives from conceptualisation through to implementation demonstrated experience in the preparation and provision of responsive, clear, and concise specialist advice, recommendations, and solutions both verbally and in writing workshop facilitation, including experience presenting information in non-technical terms to a wide variety of end-users with differing needs demonstrated understanding of relevant legislation, Australian standards and guidelines. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney  The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Deputy-Dean--Research-_0158401-3": {
      "title": "Deputy Dean (Research)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nLocated in Camperdown 5 year FIXED-TERM position with an attractive remuneration package About the opportunity The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences (FASS) at the University of Sydney has commenced its search for the key leadership role of Deputy Dean (Research). The new Deputy Dean (Research) will be joining at a key time, as the University enters the second phase of its ten-year strategic cycle (Sydney in 2032) and the Faculty progresses its own second strategic plan. The new Deputy Dean (Research) will work alongside the Dean, Professor Lisa Adkins, as FASS looks to consolidate its position as a world-leading centre for research and education across the humanities, arts, and social sciences. The Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences at Sydney is the oldest and one of the most comprehensive of its kind in Australia. The Faculty remains proud of its past while focused on the future. The Faculty’s distinctiveness lies in the depth and breadth of its disciplines, with its many research and teaching strengths earning it a deserved reputation for excellence nationally and internationally. The Faculty’s research is driven by the pursuit of multidisciplinary knowledge that benefits Australia and the wider world. In 2025 we welcomed two new flagship centres, the Centre for AI, Trust and Governance, which advances responsible AI development through multidisciplinary research, fosters public trust, and informs policy decisions in complex regulatory environments; and the Contemporary Inequalities Centre, which brings together multidisciplinary expertise to pursue collaborative, problem-driven research, aiming to respond to emerging social and economic challenges to build the evidence base needed to shape new policy responses. Our other flagship research centres include the Vere Gordon Childe Centre, and the Sydney Centre for Healthy Societies. Other research centres, institutes, and groups in the Faculty include the Sydney Institute for Community Languages Education, the CREATE Centre, and the Sydney Social Sciences and Humanities Advanced Research Centre (SSSHARC). For our students, and in collaboration with our partners, the Faculty offers internships and work placements, overseas exchange and interdisciplinary collaboration around complex, real-world problems. As we mature our connections with industry, community and government, the Faculty is seeking to establish anchoring partnerships operating across its research and education portfolios.    About you (skills, capabilities and experience) The Deputy Dean (Research) is a senior academic leader responsible for the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences research portfolio and for achieving the strategic and operational objectives in research set out in the Faculty’s strategic plan . Working in partnership with the Faculty’s Head of Research and Development, and as a key member of the Faculty leadership team, the role provides strategic leadership for the Faculty’s research agenda, driving the implementation of key research initiatives and aligning priorities, investment and capability with broader University objectives. Working closely with the Dean and key stakeholders across the Faculty, the Deputy Dean (Research) fosters a high-performing, inclusive and collaborative research culture that supports excellence, innovation and interdisciplinary impact. The role provides oversight of research centres, research infrastructure, and research performance. It strengthens research capability and capacity, supports career development, and champions research integrity and responsible research practices. As a senior representative of the Faculty, the Deputy Dean (Research) will build strong relationships across the University and with industry, government, community and global partners, identifying opportunities for funding, collaboration, translation and impact while ensuring the Faculty’s research activities remain strategically aligned, financially sustainable and positioned for long-term success. The appointee will hold a doctoral level qualification in a FASS discipline and have an excellent track record in research and research funding. They will have a proven record in research leadership at scale with demonstrated achievements in the implementation of research strategy and research performance uplift. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Visa sponsorship and relocation assistance will be provided if required. Term and remuneration The term of the appointment is five years on a full-time basis. The successful candidate will also be offered a professorial appointment in an appropriate discipline in the Faculty and will retain that position at the conclusion of their Deputy Deanship. The successful candidate will be offered a competitive remuneration package commensurate with the responsibilities of the position and the candidate’s relevant experience and qualifications. Pre-employment checks Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.  We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications ( including a CV and cover letter outlining how your skills, capabilities and experience meet the requirements for the role ) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page. Please refer to the candidate information brochure/position description for further details. Candidate Brochure: Candidate Brochure DDR Click to view the Position Description for this role. For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply. For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Jackie Besley from Devereaux Consulting at FASSResearch@devereauxconsulting.com © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Applications Close Tuesday 10 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Specialist-Psychosocial-Safety_0158081-3": {
      "title": "Senior Specialist Psychosocial Safety",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-14\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, continuing opportunity Located on Camperdown Campus on Gadigal Country Base Salary from $152,045 plus 17% superannuation About the team   The Health, Safety & Wellbeing (HSW) team plays a critical role in enabling safe, healthy and sustainable ways of working across the University. Through listening, learning and a commitment to continuous improvement, HSW promotes consistent best practice, frameworks and standards across all faculties and business areas. Together, the team helps shape a culture where health, safety and wellbeing are embedded into everyday work, supporting organisational excellence and long-term success. About the opportunity As Senior Specialist, Psychosocial Safety , you will provide expert advice, guidance, and support across all aspects of the University’s operations and academic activities. With a strong focus on complex matters and critical risks, you will work collaboratively across functions including HSW Partnering, HR Partnering and Staff Health Support, to support the effective management and mitigation of identified psychosocial risks. This includes coaching local leaders to strengthen safety controls and embed practices that prevent future harm. Working closely with the HSW Partnering team, you will ensure proportionate risk mitigation is provided to Faculties, Schools, Portfolios and Business Units, uplifting partnering capability in how they support their client groups. In collaboration with the Psychosocial Safety, Health and Wellbeing team, you will deliver scalable prevention initiatives that support risk management and connect business areas with relevant support and solutions. Your key responsibilities will be to: provide expert psychosocial safety advice on complex matters and critical incidents analyse and interpret data to deliver meaningful and easily translatable insights that support local areas to proactively manage psychosocial risk continually mature the Health and Safety Management System (HSMS) in line with emerging legislative standards and best practice partner with HSW Teams and leadership to embed the psychosocial HSMS architecture across diverse University portfolios support institutional reporting and leadership capability uplift foster a comprehensive, systems-based approach to safety through robust worker consultation, strong cross functional relationships, and regulatory alignment. About you post-graduate qualifications in Psychology or Occupational Health and Safety or an equivalent combination of training and experience accreditation / registration with a relevant health or wellbeing professional association / organisation, or legislative body expert knowledge of risk management practices and the application of government legislation, codes of practice and Australian standards as they relate to psychosocial risk project management experience and ability to work on multiple projects and activities according to agreed schedules, timelines, and deliverables with minimal supervision and to prepare progress reports for management, project managers and clients demonstrated experience at a senior level in the preparation and provision of responsive, clear, and concise specialist advice, recommendations, and solutions both verbally and in writing, through effective research, negotiation, and consultation with key stakeholders demonstrated experience in developing an effective and sustainable safety culture and of conceptualising, developing, and implementing practical compliance and safety programs demonstrated experience in providing leadership, mentoring and guidance to senior staff to enable them to lead cultural and behavioural change. Sponsorship / work rights for Australia Please note: You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment. Pre-employment checks and declarations  Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment. EEO statement   At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTIQ+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds. We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Gold employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .  How to apply Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.  We understand that everyone applies differently. If you’d prefer to have an informal conversation before applying, we welcome you to get in touch so we can talk through the role and process. In addition, if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Sade Mintah via sade.mintah@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 01 November 2026 11:59 PM"
