    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Camperdown-Campus/Research-Fellow--Heat-and-Health-Research-Centre_0157988": {
      "title": "Research Fellow- Heat and Health Research Centre",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time, 3.5-year fixed-term opportunity Advance research in heat and health Base Salary $132,533 - $157,381 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   Join the Heat and Health Research Centre at the University of Sydney and contribute to world-leading research addressing the growing health challenges associated with a warming world.   As a Research Fellow, you will apply and further develop your expertise across research methodology, experimental design and delivery, contributing to innovative research that strengthens the University’s standing as a national leader in the field. Working under broad direction, you will have the opportunity to exercise your subject matter expertise, provide research advice and recommendations, and independently resolve complex issues across your research projects   You will work both independently and collaboratively within a fast-paced multidisciplinary research environment, building partnerships and translating research into real-world outcomes that can inform policy, practice and community health. This role offers an opportunity to develop your research profile while contributing to a collaborative culture committed to research excellence and meaningful impact.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   Lead and contribute to high-quality original research in climate and environmental physiology, including experimental research, physiological assessment, data analysis and statistical modelling. Build and maintain a strong research profile through high-quality publications, conference presentations and other scholarly outputs, independently and collaboratively. Develop research collaborations and partnerships with academic, government, industry and community stakeholders to translate research findings into improved policy, practice and community outcomes. Contribute to competitive research funding applications and support the delivery of research projects that generate meaningful research impact. Apply expertise in experimental and physiological research methods, including venipuncture and blood analysis, to support rigorous, high-quality and innovative research. Contribute to the research culture and strategic priorities of the Heat and Health Research Centre through mentoring, postgraduate supervision, knowledge exchange and active participation in University and centre activities and initiatives.   About you   A PhD in Environmental physiology, physiology, sports and exercise science, with demonstrated experience conducting original research and contributing to high-quality scholarly outputs. Demonstrated expertise in climate and environmental physiology, experimental and physiological assessment, data analysis and statistical modelling, with experience in venipuncture and blood analysis highly regarded. An emerging research profile, including evidence of publications, presentations, research collaborations and a developing network of academic, industry and professional stakeholders. Proven ability to communicate research effectively to academic, industry and broader audiences, together with experience contributing to presentations, forums, seminars or conferences. Demonstrated ability to plan and deliver research activities effectively, while providing supervision, mentoring and guidance to students or less experienced researchers. Proven ability to work collaboratively as part of a multidisciplinary team of researchers, while upholding high professional standards and contributing positively to a collegial and supportive team culture.     Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   This position is designated as involving child-related work. To undertake or remain in this position, you are required to apply for and obtain a Working with Children Check clearance in accordance with the Child Protection (Working with Children) Act 2012.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Sunday 15 November 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Newtown/Centre-Manager-Centre-for-Disability-Research-Policy_0157856": {
      "title": "Centre Manager-Centre for Disability Research Policy",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull-time Continuing opportunity, located at Camperdown Campus Exciting opportunity for a Centre Manager to join the Centre for Disability Research and Policy Base Salary $131,108 - $147,858 + 17% superannuation     About the opportunity   The Centre for Disability Research and Policy produces collaborative research that actively influences policy and practice to improve the lives of people with disability in Australia and the Asia-Pacific and aims to reduce the disadvantage that occurs for people with disability. The Centre's research works to improve the social and economic participation, health and wellbeing of people with disability. We have an exciting opportunity for a Centre Manager to provide operational leadership for the Centre for Disability Research and Policy to meet its core objective whilst also supporting the Centre and School and Faculty’s strategic research agenda.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   collaboratively work with the Centre Director, Senior academic leaders and colleagues in the Centre for Disability Research and Policy to lead the Centre play a key role in overall business planning and implementation, monitoring and reporting drive improvement in process and overall performance and manage the workforce and operational resources, ensuring centre operations, communications, and events adhere to leading accessibility and inclusion standards deliver on budget and financial management support and manage/prepare the Centre’s budget and work collaboratively with FMH Finance provide strategic direction to ensure the Centre’s programs achieve their aims and operational objectives initiate and develop long-term relationships with internal and external stakeholders manage governance and compliance including committee membership, reporting and communications   About you   postgraduate qualifications in the field of disability, including disabled people's organisations, government, and community partners, with proven experience in research management with the ability to work on multiple projects and adapt to changes in line with overall strategies a well-developed understanding of the Australian disability sector policy landscape and the principles of co-design, with lived experience of disability viewed favorably extensive operational and administrative leadership, possessing the capability to translate strategic goals into operational initiatives advanced interpersonal, verbal and written communication skills with sound negotiating and conflict resolution skills and a demonstrated level of tact and discretion in dealing with day-to-day operational matters and stakeholders in the research process excellent oral and written communication skills and the ability to liaise effectively with all levels of staff, students, management and external stakeholders  financial management experience and literacy, experience in management budgets and controlling income and expenditure hold strong analytical, problem solving and time management skills.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.   Pre-employment checks and declarations     Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard. Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Roshik Prasad, Recruitment Operations by email to roshik.prasad@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Monday 26 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    },
    "https://usyd.wd105.myworkdayjobs.com/USYD_EXTERNAL_CAREER_SITE/job/Darlington-Campus/Senior-Systems-Consultant_0157936-2": {
      "title": "Senior Systems Consultant",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 3 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nFull time fixed term opportunity until January 2028    Utilise your expertise to provide support for Human Resource Workforce Systems users across the University through ensuring usability, data integrity, accuracy and compliance Base Salary $131,108 + 17% superannuation About the opportunity   The primary purpose of this role is to lead continuous improvement projects and system support ensuring usability, data integrity, accuracy, and compliance. This position will define system requirements and specifications and be Responsible for assigned testing activities with minimal supervision including participation in identifying scenarios to be included in the test execution. Working in the systems team, the successful candidate will interact with system vendors, solution architects, business analysts, business users, and management. Candidates should possess strong project skills and be able to work autonomously with little supervision. Experience in using the Workday or Ascender Pay system is highly desirable.   What are we looking for?   Ideal candidates for this role will be experienced in system analysis, design and implementation of information systems or continuous improvement initiatives within a large organisation. You will possess excellent communication skills and problem-solving ability within a business environment. We are looking for people who can build and maintain positive working relationships to influence key stakeholders and work consultatively to facilitate the delivery of quality outcomes.   Your key responsibilities will be to:   provide day to day support to the Human Resource group as well as University managers and staff in the use of the Human Resource Information System ensuring compliance with HR policies and procedures design and supply tools and materials to support Human Resource staff and other end users to effectively utilise the existing Human Resource systems for their needs, including training as needed to increase utilisation of self-service functions ensure approval flows and automated system notifications are configured correctly to meet Human Resource policy and procedure requirements in accordance with the formal delegations of authority coordinate and organise user acceptance testing, roll out of system patches, fixes and updates to minimise disruptions, and communicate system improvements, updates and outages across the University conduct detailed research and analysis as part of the broader Human Resources agenda and work with the reporting and analytics team to ensure system optimisation and data accuracy and integrity. About you   post graduate qualifications in a relevant field or an equivalent combination of training and experience demonstrated experience working with a large, entity wide HRIS dealing with extraction, analysis and maintenance of data experience managing or participating in HR system implementations, upgrades or system projects previous experience in a shared service environment (HR or operational) experience in delivering training to support the implementation or alteration to HRIS systems or processes previous experience working in a Higher Education environment or large organisations.     Sponsorship / work rights for Australia   You must have unrestricted work rights in Australia for the duration of this employment to be eligible to apply. Visa sponsorship is not available for this appointment.     Pre-employment checks and declarations       Your employment is conditional upon the successful completion of all pre-employment or background checks required for the role in terms satisfactory to the University.  Also, to meet the University’s obligations under the National Higher Education Code to Prevent and Eliminate Gender-Based Violence you will be asked to declare if you have been investigated for, or found to engaged in, sexual harm or gender-based violence in the course of previous employment or in a legal process. Similarly, your ongoing employment is conditional upon the satisfactory maintenance of all relevant clearances and background check requirements. If you do not meet these conditions, the University may take any necessary step, including the termination of your employment.   EEO statement     At the University of Sydney, our shared values are trust, accountability and excellence and we strive to be a place where everyone can thrive. We are committed to creating a University community that thrives through diversity and reflects the wider community that we serve. We deliver on this through our commitment to diversity and inclusion , evidenced by our people and culture programs, as well as key strategies to increase participation and support the careers of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander People , women , people living with a disability , people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds , and those who identify as LGBTQIA+ . We welcome applications from candidates from all backgrounds.    We are proud to be recognised as an Australian Workplace Equality Index (AWEI) Platinum Employer. Find out more about our work on diversity and inclusion .    How to apply   Applications (including a cover letter, CV, and any additional supporting documentation) can be submitted via the Apply button at the top of the page.     For employees of the University or contingent workers, please login into your Workday account and navigate to the Career icon on your Dashboard.  Click on USYD Find Jobs and apply.   For a confidential discussion about the role, or if you require reasonable adjustment or any documents in alternate formats, please contact Victoria Ford-Rogers Recruitment Operations by email to victoria.ford-rogers@sydney.edu.au   © The University of Sydney   The University reserves the right not to proceed with any appointment. Click to view the Position Description for this role. Applications Close Tuesday 20 October 2026 11:59 PM"
    }
  },
